g? Yeah. Yeah. And so some of the newer, I don't know if you went to the Pro series, but those frames didn't do that so much, but they were also kind of narrow and you'd strap the bag on and then it would have this sort of lateral movement on the frame. It didn't just like lock down totally. So the pack kind of barreled on it because the frame was so narrow? Yeah. So it just had that attachment point there. So if you had a load back there, it would kind of like wobble along sometimes and it was kind of hard to get really locked down. At least it was for me. But that's completely solved with this new frame. It really, you could really lock stuff down on it. And loading the load sling, they call it, is easy to do and you can just lock that sucker in. When I put the test quarter on there, I was able to just get it totally tight against the frame. Sounds awesome. I know that they do a lot of innovative bag designs where they really have a purpose with their layout. Are the bags interchangeable on this frame system? Yeah. So all of the Icon 6 bags are interchangeable with the same frame. And they go from a 3500, which is the sort of large day pack size that I tested, all the way up to an 8000. I would really like to test one of the larger cubic packs because the frame has tons of potential there. And I really like the 3500. I do a ton of day hunting in our area, especially for elk and the rifle season. But I do like to do overnights and multi-days. I just got back from a multi-day trip and I used the Icon 3500 and it just was, it was difficult to get all of the stuff I needed into that 3500 cube pack. But I mean, it worked. And I did some overnights during scouting season. And that worked fine, actually, for overnights. It was fine, especially for scouting because you don't have your weapon system and some of the other gear that you have during the season. But I'm interested to see one of the larger bags. I've always been a fan of Kuyu bags as far as like they have a lot