t. They were more comfortable than the Canetrex. But they were still a little tight on the width. Then I made them work. And, you know, unless I did a big day or something. I wasn't getting blisters or anything like that. It was just, they were just snug. So I got a hold of Stephen this spring when they got their super wides in and just said, what do you think about me running this boat? He had me take some measurements on my foot. Now, the first time we did it, I did, what's that called, Stephen Abratic? That's, yeah, that's her foot mapping kit. Yeah. So we went through the foot mapping kit. And Stephen didn't have me do that again because he had it on file. But he said, just take a measurement of your foot across the width of your foot and send me a picture. So I did. And he said, man, you are totally a candidate for the super wides. Your feet are even wider than they were in 2013. And I just happened to know this too. I had been buying tens the last four or five years and everything, tennis shoes and stuff like that. My foot has just gotten bigger. And so Stephen wanted me to go to a 10 and a half in these super wides, which I was still a little like, gosh, I'm going to be wearing a freaking clawed hopper here. So I got the boots. I've had them since July. And this is what I found. The other boots that I was wearing, they were still too tight. I didn't realize it until I got into your super wide. And it wasn't that there's slop in there at all. It's just now the boot actually fits my whole foot. My foot is very wide across the toes, the widest part. And that was instantly noticeable when I started wearing the boot. And then the heel lock, I always thought a heel lock meant tight in the heel. And so that's why I thought the other boots were doing fine. Well, in this boot, it's not really tight in the heel, but I don't get a lot of slippage. Like there's room for my heel back there. And so I've worn them for two months now. Now, you know, I'm a horse hunter. I don't do 10 mile days, but, you know, I do three mile days. And I love them. They fit my feet so much better that I thought, man, I got to get Stephen on here. I know there's a lot of big footed guys out there that probably think if they're just buying a 4E and going up a half a size, they're doing fine. But Stephen, something you explained to me is that there's some technology behind the wides. They're not just a bigger boot, right? Right. Yeah, that's correct. Why don't you take that from there? So the big thing is... How old is that buck or bull you tagged this season? Find out at Mattson's Lab. We'll tell you exactly how old your big game animal is, just like we've done for thousands of hunters since 1969. Mattson's Lab ages deer, elk, bear, and other species with stunning accuracy. It's simple. Just send us a tooth and we'll deliver your age results right to your inbox. Age your game at mattsonslab.com. Because every season tells a story and every animal has an age. From Lattson's perspective, when we're fitting feet, just in general, we look at the different boots and the different widths as a tool. And we don't use a narrow or a last and then make a 2E. And a lot of companies will take a standard last and they're barely given any width, but they'll make it a little deeper because it's easier for them to do. And so you're really not getting a true fit. Well, what we've done is when we make a standard, which is a D width, it's a D width. When we make a 2E width, it's a 2E width. There's literally expansion of the last, which is the plastic, for a lack of a better term, it's the model that the boot is built over. And then when that model is pulled out, you have that much volume to stick your foot in. And there's a couple ways to cheat it, like you were talking about. Go with a 2E width and jump up a half size. Yeah, you gain a little length, you gain a little more width. Well, what we finally did, and it took a lot of work on our part, is we developed our super wide. And this super wide boot is perfect for folks that have got a Fred Flintstone foot. Like, I know it's kind of funny to say that, but their big toe and their pinky toe are almost on the same plane. Their foot resembles a shoebox, but it's very squared off in the toe area. It doesn't matter what they put on, this person will typically feel pressure around their pinky toe, the side of the big toe, or the fourth toe. They do, they find softer, more pliable footwear to feel more comfortable on them. And what we were able to do is to develop a boot last to accommodate that style of foot, but truly still capture the midfoot, which is the arch area, and still capture the back of the heel through that padding. And it was funny, you mentioned your heel's not slipping, but there's room back there. So when you're underfit in a boot, that's zero tolerance. And when we climb up a mountain, even though you're only doing three miles, you still got to walk up the side of a mountain. There's elongation that takes place from digging your toe and your knees going over the front of your foot. Your foot elongates, there's no space for it to go. So it's going to fit a little tighter. You're going to say, well, my heel's locked in. Well, the reality is you've got zero tolerance there. So you're basically just driving the back of the heel and the back of the boot. Well, what we're able to do is to drop down considerable sizing through that foot. So we're giving somebody a standard width in the midfoot, a standard width in the heel, but we're able to give them that wide forefoot. So they really have still got that performance fit, right? And a lot of these brands, they make them wide in the front. They're wide in the arch and they're wide in the heel. It's like just sliding their foot in it. And there's really no anatomical padding, this padding around the back to really hold that boot securely on the foot. And that's what you meant when you said, look, this isn't just a bigger boot. It's designed to still fit the foot everywhere it needs to fit and accommodate the super wide. Yeah. And it's been, like I said, it's a tool. You know, we've got our standard, our wide fit, and then we've got our super wide. And there's actually an area on our website when you land on the page and you scroll down, I don't know, maybe halfway. It says different, different widths for all foot shapes, boot last comparison. And you click it. And we have really spent a lot of time building this to where someone can identify the standard. And then we have the wide fit where it, I think I may have emailed that to you to try to explain it to you in the spring. Maybe not. But it shows the wide fit over the top of the standard. And then we show the super wide over the top of the standard. So a person that's trying to learn this can really identify their own foot shape. And then when they identify their own foot shape, then they can look at the models, which it's kind of funny because when you go to other boot websites, they're going to filter out their boots by what you're going to use it for and the rest of this stuff. And I mean, that's all fine and dandy. But at the end of the day, these mountain boots, at least like what we're building, they're really supportive, right? They got to fit right or they're not going to feel good. So we categorize our boots and our drop down as mountain boots, but we categorize them by width to help get that person going the right way before they ever pick one, right? Because if they read somebody that's wearing a pair of boots or like, man, that's the one I want to buy. But that's not always the best boot for that person, right? And that's why I wanted to pull you on here because I think there's a lot of guys that, and I was one of them, we used to buy them off the shelf when there's brick and mortar stores. Now it's even worse. We just buy them off of the internet. Right. And you can skate doing that. You can get by with that for years. And I did. I did. But that was why I wanted to bring people on this journey a little bit that while those other boots worked and they fit well enough, these super wides, because of going through the custom boot process and just the way you've built the super wides to still anatomically fit the foot, not just blow out a boot, make it bigger. I'm in such a better place than I was. I've got a boot that fits me now. And I wanted people to, you know, we didn't say it because I'm assuming they know, but later in the summer, you guys are, your dad's a podiatrist. You guys have worked under that for years. They're not just selling boots. They're fitting boots to your feet. If you're having to buy it off the internet, we won't stop you. But I'm just saying if you have any foot issues at all, hunting, blisters, anything. And by the way, I wasn't even getting all that stuff. I just didn't have a great fitting boot. That there's a lot more that goes into this than just getting your boot off of what size you've been buying for the last 20 years. A hundred percent right. And we have some people that don't necessarily need to go through the custom process. They just want to pick up the phone and have a person actually try to help them. I mean, let's get real. $500 to $600 investment for a pair of boots is a lot. I don't know about you. I want to pick up the phone and talk to someone to kind of help guide me through it. And at that point, they may realize, you know what? After talking with either Steven or James or one of the other guys here, they may say, you know, I was going to spend $500 or $600 on a boot. But dude, after talking to you, spending the extra $500 to have it done once and have a chart set up is probably a better option for me because I struggle with everything. Well, it was for me because my tech lights worked fine. But that was, what, 12 years ago? I was 45 then. And just, I mean, I didn't know you had that stuff on file. When I started getting old of you, you were just like, hey, I've got this stuff. We went through it. But just take a measurement on your foot so we can update this. And that's when you moved me to the 10 and a half. So to me, that was the value in the custom boot process. Because Steven, I read on the forum, guys, oh, you're too expensive. Okay, fine. If you're not having any foot problems, don't spend it. Don't do it. Don't do it. But if you're having any issues at all, stop just buying boots off the internet and go through the custom fit process. You'll have the information for life. And then, this is the question I was going to ask you. Steven, why do my, like, dude, I weigh the same as I did 12 years ago. Why are my feet getting bigger? Right. So this is interesting. And this was always the case when we were in, it was still practicing in the podiatry or the pediatric room. I'm going to tell you, there's a shift. Now, we all know when our children are growing their feet, they hit that growth spurt, right? Then they kind of mellow out. Some are late bloomers, so it lasts longer. But I would say there's another shift. And it's somewhere in the 30s. You're in your 30s is when another shift typically takes place. But then we hit another shift. And it's when we're in our late 40s and into our 50s. I've experienced the same thing. And part of the reason is, I believe this to be true. We'll say this. Hereditary. Certain foot types, when you reach a certain age, these abnormalities show up, right? Foot deformities, bummions, things like that, right? We always remember grandma or grandpa having a bad foot. Well, you're finally getting some of that. You're just hitting your point in life. So, there's the hereditary. Then there's the use. Like, if we have a job or our hobbies are really hard on our feet, orthopedic injuries, things like that, those can also cause flattening of the foot. But as we get older, we lose fat pad on the foot. Fat pad fills the void within a boot. So, a boot fit will change, right? And more than what we see is the elongation. The foot just, the joint's starting to settle. So, that's really what happens over time. And I know a lot of people will say, I'm just going to throw an arch support in the boot. But the fact of the matter is, is when you're 50, 57, 55, and you throw an arch support in the boot, you ain't jacking that arch up. You're hurting that guy. You aren't driving that arch. You're not 12 years old anymore, man. When your foot flattens out, that foot's pretty damn fixed. Like, it's not going to want to come back up. And you're going to find it to be relatively uncomfortable when you start doing stuff like that. Gotcha. So, it's pretty normal for a lot of people just for their foot to just, you said elongate. Do you mean? Yeah, it elongates. And wider. Yeah, it does. Because as it elongates, it splays as well, right? The foot widens out. And if you've already got a wider foot to begin with, it just becomes more noticeable, too. And, you know, our body's natural fat pad is a protective layer to keep us from getting hot spots and blistering. So, you will also see as we get older, we lose that fat pad around our digits, the back of the heel, things like that. And the skin's thinner. And it's more prone to blistering. And when you really notice it, you won't notice it in an athletic shoe or a more flexible boot, right? It's when you get into footwear that does have structure. It does have some torsional stability to it. That's when you're getting resistance applied to the foot in that fit, in that imperfection, and the fit really shines. So, you really got to have it right. So, the fact that you're able, I mean, honest to God, you should flash up a picture of your bare feet so people can go, shit, now I know what they're talking about because they're wide. And, I mean, they're hard to fit feet, man. They just are. And the fact that you're able to put our boot on is as stiff as that boot is, as supportive as that boot is, and really, like, sing the praises the way that you are about it. That's a testament to what we've built. That's no kidding, man. Because that boot will eat your lunch if that boot is not fit, right? And I didn't mention that in my intro. My podiatrist, I was getting some ankle pain. And in 2021, he diagnosed me with arthritis in those bones that are just right below the crease of your ankle. I don't even remember what they're called. But, you know, where your ankle flexes and then you move towards the toes. He says, you're just getting some arthritis in there. He said, quit wearing Crocs and light tennis shoes. Make sure you have a substantial boot when you're hunting. You know, because, you know, back then I was getting away with, you know, lighter stuff. And, you know, I could wear tennis shoes and go bow hunting. But he said, welcome to your 50s. He says, you got to take better and more of your feet. And it was so funny, Stephen. I ended up going to an orthopedic for my knee. Gosh, all this old man stuff here. I don't know, like a year or two later. And I was mentioning my ankle. And he says, hey, we got you in here. Let me just x-ray. And he x-rayed. Oh, you got bones, bird. You got that. You got that. We can hack on it. We can go in there and operate on you. And I ended up not going through that. And just follow. The reason I'm telling you all that. Just following what the old podiatrist said. He's probably retired now. He was that close. Just wearing a better boot. Dude, my ankle is. It's better now than it was then. I have less ankle pain now. Now, if I run around in Crocs and tennis shoes and all that stuff too much, then it comes right back. And that was the other reason I wanted to go to a stiffer boot. And this boot is. The Kennetrex were fine. But they were like overstuffed. This boot has enough flex in it that I. It's not like a tennis shoe flex. But it's a little less than what I had in the Crispy Summit 2s. But to me, it's just right. It's not overstuffed. So I just wanted to put that in there so guys don't think that, you know, we're going to something that's too stiff. No, it's not. The huge thing that James and I have both done is we engineered that boot to where it can be relasted. So that means that the boot can be warmed to about 145 degrees. We have tooling for this. We can go in and reshape that boot and let it cool and hold its shape. Those are thermal set reinforcements and they're behind the leather. There's foam applied to that. And then you have the waterproof membrane. So that is what truly stabilizes the stiffness there. And then the full length shank of that boot. It does have some spring in it. And the way that it's built is using a series of layers of nylon and fiberglass. It's built up in a mold and it's varying degrees of thickness. So you get the support where you want it and a little bit of spring. And to your point, as much of an advocate as I am for, you know, a very functional stiff boot, there is something to be said that I have just a little bit. A little bit of spring in the toe of it, you know, to where you really don't feel like you're just, you know, clunking around in the boot. Yeah, yeah, exactly. That's what I felt like in the Kentrex. I was just a little clunky, harder to be quiet, stuff like that. And then the last thing I wanted to say about them, and I know this is probably low on a lot of people's lists, but they're just a good looking boot, you know, with that red color in and, you know, the smooth leather. When I was doing the review, I just thought, man, this is just a nice looking boot. Everywhere I've worn them, I always get compliments on them. So if people care about that stuff, they got the good look mojo going. If people want to see the pictures of my feet, like Stephen suggested, if you just go to the Rock Slide Footwear Subforum, it's pinned at the top. So this is an ongoing review. I'm just posting to that thread as I get more time on them. And I've got pictures of my feet in there. And you'll see what I mean when I said I have them. Well, they're wide. They're wide. They're wide. They're wide. I know, dude. It's embarrassing. Got to get this big mule deer, man. And then just as we wrap it up, even if you don't have wide feet, if you're having any foot issues at all and you want to get the best fit in boot you can, give Stephen or James a call at Lathrop & Sons and consider going through their custom boot process. And they'll be able to help you with every pair of boots from here on out. And you'll learn a lot about your feet. I know I did. It'd be awesome. We'd be happy to help you. Robbie, I've got to touch on one thing. I completely forgot. James would kill me if I didn't bring this up. But I'm going to say about this time last year, my brother James spent probably close to, I'd say, two months working with a group and started submitting the different footwear that we have under our range of boots to get HSA approval. It's a big deal. He got it done. What's it? It's a health savings account. So there are certain employers that provide a credit card. There's withholdings taken out of it and applied to that. The cool thing is this. It is a health benefit. Therefore, that can be used. Now, I think HSA, FSA, so there's a little different way it has to be submitted with that. James has all the details on it. But the facts are this. The boots were looked at with this panel. They wanted to look at this footwear. They wanted to see why it was beneficial, why it should apply to that. There are specific issues with the foot that this footwear, while it's non-prescription, not prescription, non-prescription items, it is a benefit to those problems. So, you know, arch pain, heel pain, hammer toes, bunions. I mean, you name it, if you're experiencing that or someone has been diagnosed with that, this footwear, there are actual articles written about some of the components that we have integrated into our boots and those benefits to those issues. So you can go through it. And this is not only the footwear. It's off-the-shelf footwear. It's our custom boot system process. It's the mapping kit that is used to get your feet here. Are you with me? Yeah, so I don't have an HSA right now, but I did for about four years. And if I remember right, you could put money in it tax-free, no tax on it. And, you know, so it depends on your tax bracket. It was like getting 30% on the dollar for where I was. And so if I put a dollar in, I'd have $1.30 I could spend. And then it had to be spent on qualifying purchases, you know, old man stuff, eyeglasses, you know. I like what you're saying. So what you're saying, if somebody has an HSA, you guys can point them in the right direction. And it's possible they can get, just say, to pay for any of these services and even a pair of boots. Boots or the custom foot, the whole custom boot system, even foot orthotics, they'll cover that. It has to be done through us. It has to be through our process. They've approved our process. They have approved the way that we're approaching and the fitment of them. They can do it online. It's all right there. There's a link right on our website. It says at the top, check out the HSA-approved products. And they hit it. And, I mean, it's all right there. The range of boots, the custom boot system. We've even got some training shoes and athletic shoes that are approved now. Our synergy insoles that we pour in here, some compression sleeves, blister prevention. It is worth somebody taking a look at. If they are spending any amount of time in boots, mountain hunting, doing these things, we've got those people covered. And I just don't know how you could beat that opportunity, to be honest with you. Yeah, you've got an HSA, man. It's a no-brainer. Cool, man. Cool. Glad you mentioned that. I didn't even know about it. Okay, my man. We'll catch you next time. Thank you, Betty. Bye-bye. All right, moving on. Colorado's right to hunt and fish constitutional amendment update. We got Grant Jerry back to fill us in what's happening as we speak. So, we're getting closer and closer to the election day. And we just want to stay well informed. So, welcome back, Grant. Hey, Sam. Thanks for having me back on. Like you said, we just wanted to kind of do a quick update. As there has been some changes, what was previously Amendment 84, Colorado constitutional right to hunt and fish. It has been changed to Colorado Amendment 83. Essentially, one of the other ballot initiatives dropped off, and so they had to move it up. So, going forward, you know, everything, whether it be campaign material or you look for it on the ballot or you look for it in the blue book, you need to look for Colorado Amendment 83, which was certified, which enshrines the right to hunt and fish in the Colorado Constitution. It was originally Initiative 302. And essentially, at its core, this measure would make Colorado the 25th state in the United States to constitutionally protect those outdoor traditions. So, it's basically just to protect our heritage of hunting and fishing for conservation. And it's nothing new. A lot of places have adopted it. You know, this will be half of America, 25 states. Yeah, so 24 states currently have that constitutional right to hunt and fish. You know, upon successful passing in November, Colorado would be the 25th state. And yeah, and it's really clear to, you know, to say, you know, what it will do, you know, provide that constitutional protection for our rich hunting and fishing heritage, a permanent right to hunt and fish, harvest wildlife. And it'll really move those protections from state statute, where it kind of currently exists, into the state constitution, which really just provides some guardrails, some additional hurdles for folks if they're trying to, you know, eliminate hunting or fishing or, you know, move to, you know, significant restrictions in that going forward. Really just to provide some protections there and really some guardrails for whether it be the Wildlife Commission, the legislature, et cetera. And it really kind of, it designates hunting and fishing as the state's preferred method for managing and controlling wildlife populations and really implementing that while those wildlife conservation principles, which we do currently, and then safeguarding some of those standard traditional methods that have been in use for quite some time. Legal firearms, archery equipment, muzzleloaders, standard fishing and rods and reels and things like that. So really, like I said, safeguarding that, putting that into the constitution. Along with that, really some of the more, you know, important things too is what does it not change? Because there is a lot of information in the media space. So it doesn't change the authority of Colorado Parks and Wildlife. They retain full regulatory authority to set seasons, bag limits, method of take restrictions, licensing requirements, all those things. That remains unchanged. It does not grant permission to trespass, to go on private property. And then it provides exemptions for protected wildlife. So these constitutional protections, it doesn't apply to endangered species or non-gamed species or any wildlife that doesn't fall under the purview of Colorado Parks and Wildlife. And then one of the really important pieces is it doesn't overturn any previously banned practices. So, you know, various bans that were passed in the 1990s regarding trapping or bear hunting practices, it doesn't overturn those or change any of those upon successful passage of Amendment 83. And I think that's really important as we, you know, one of the other things that's probably changed since the last time we talked is we have seen some opposition groups really kind of start to hit the airways and start to push a lot of what I would say, you know, false or misleading information regarding what Amendment 83 is and what it isn't. Some of them even kind of masquerading as sportsmen and women and seeking to convince folks that maybe hunters and anglers aren't necessarily on board with Amendment 83, which nothing could be farther from the truth. If you pay attention to, you know, a lot of your premier wildlife conservation organizations, Rocky Mountain Elk Foundation, Backcountry Hunters and Anglers, Wild Sheet Foundation, all those groups have thrown their support behind Colorado Amendment 83 because they do believe it is the best way to really enshrine our hunting and fishing heritage. And really that was wildlife conservation principles that we hold so dear. Yeah, I do think, you know, like we talked about throughout this whole time, Colorado really is the testing grounds. Everybody's getting geared up. There's a lot more money being thrown around right before the election trying to sway people. And I think you just really have to stay true to yourself, get out there, talk with your neighbors, people in your circle, those that we're close to and make sure that, like you talked about, people understand what it is and what it isn't. And then they can make an informed decision on how they feel about Amendment 83. Yeah, I mean, I would implore all, you know, hunters and anglers, particularly here in Colorado, but really nationwide, you know, to talk to your neighbors, your friends, your colleagues at work, the folks you go to church with. And, you know, and, you know, and, you know, really kind of explain, you know, what hunting and fishing does, you know, what it means to you and your family. And then also, you know, what it means as far as solidifying that, that strong conservation ethics and that stewardship of the landscape. And have those people, you know, talk to how Amendment 83 would just ensure that those things continue on in perpetuity and kind of spreading that message at the really grassroots level. You know, we'll see people, there'll be plenty of, like, yard signs and TV commercials and social media things. But what I think is really impactful and what we really learned from Proposition 127 is there's no substitute for really passionate individuals talking plainly and in good faith with their friends and their neighbors and their colleagues about what hunting and fishing means to them. Yeah, if it's important to you, get involved. Now is the time. It's not going to come around again. You're not going to have regrets if you don't get involved. And it could be super simple. Grant, why don't you remind us of some of the ways that, you know, people can just get on social media and show their support? Yeah, so there's kind of two main organizations that are running this campaign. So one's the International Order of T. Roosevelt. They have a website, yeson83co.org. So you can go there, find all the various campaign materials, their contact information. And then they have a really useful tool called yescohuntfish.org. So you go to that site and you can create some really cool graphics that you can put on your Facebook, Instagram, TikTok, whatever social media you choose. Just a good graphic, letting everybody know and letting your followers and your friends and your contacts there that you support Colorado Amendment 83 and why hunting and fishing is important to you. I mean, then at the state level, you have the Coloradans for Responsible Wildlife Management. You know, local organizations have really been in the fight to protect hunting for quite some time. I think going on almost five years and you can go to their various social media sites, SaveTheHuntColorado.com is their website. And then they have another sister website, WildlifeDeserveBetter.com, which was really one of their kind of key tagline as they were fighting against the mountain lion hunting band Prop 127 back in 2024. Yeah, I think, you know, they've really proven themselves throughout the fight that they really are an ally of the sportsmen there. And, you know, Dan Gates has been working his tail off trying to get that information and keeping it flowing there. And I applaud all of you guys out there that just, you know, got your head in the fight there. And I hope everybody listening, just take the time and at least have some of these conversations with your neighbors and those in your community and inform people. And then they can make an informed decision, too. And if we do that, you know, there's no downside to Constitutional Amendment 83. Yeah, absolutely. Like I said, folks can get involved at the ground level, you know, talking with their friends. Then, you know, obviously, if you're so inclined, you can donate financially, SaveTheHuntColorado.com. I will tell you that that money really works at the ground level. You know, somebody like me that, you know, you attend Colorado Parks and Wildlife Commission meetings, whether it be Senate or Colorado House committee meetings, those CRWM individuals are always there. And they're always there sticking up for our hunters and anglers and our ability to, you know, sustainably harvest wildlife in the future. All right, Grant, I'll get that all linked up there in the show notes. Now's the time to get involved, people. And I think if we have a good show in there in November, we'll have the 25th state. So I appreciate all you do over there, Grant. If people want to ask more questions or get involved, go to some of these websites. And yeah, a lot of information out there. Thanks, Sam. Appreciate it. If you've enjoyed the show, please leave a comment wherever you listen to the Rockcast. Your reviews ensure the Rock... If you enjoyed the show, please leave a comment wherever you listen to the Rockcast. Your reviews ensure the Rockcast remains free and continues to attract great guests. If you have a guest you'd love to hear on the show or a piece of gear you'd love to see reviewed, please reach out to me, Sam, at RockSlide.com. Until next time, this has been Sam Weaver. Across the West, the Mule Deer Foundation and Blacktail Deer Foundation are restoring habitat, improving migration corridors, and reducing wildfire risk. Head over to MuleDeer.org or BlackTailDeer.org to join the herd and receive $5 off your membership with the code ROCKSLIDE. That's R-O-K-S-L-I-D-E. Or purchase a dual membership for $60 and save $10. Together we can conserve the future of these Western icons and put more deer on the landscape. Music Music Music Music Music Music  
