## Item text
Welcome to the Monday Minute of the Hunt Backcountry Podcast. These are shorter and more informal episodes where we answer your listener questions. And today I am running solo to answer some of your listener questions. Steve is out and we had some questions, you know, really piling up in email, which is a great thing. But also some specifically either to me or just that I could talk about fully. Um, so I just thought, hey, Steve's out. Let's still do this Monday Minute and get to your questions. Most of these will be gear related. Um, some of them are, I would say, gear specific on recommendations, but also there's like strategies on the best way to use gear or deal with certain conditions as well. Before we dive into the first one, if you have any questions for us for a future episode, send an email to podcast at exomountaingear.com. It'd be great to hear from you. We can cover anything from gear to tactics, strategy, whatever, whatever you want to hear. Let us know. Send that question in and we'll get to it on a future Monday Minute. All right, let's just get into it. The first one's from Camden and he said, did you use down or synthetic on your recent Alaska hunt? And how about merino or synthetic? I'm curious what you choose for Alaska conditions in general, and if a trip with packrafting also influences your decision. Any other tips you have for staying warm and dry on a trip like that would be appreciated. Good questions, Camden. And for context for listeners, we were in Alaska for 10 days at the beginning of September. And as Camden mentioned, it was a packraft trip. So we covered about 50 river miles, floated that basically in three different stretches, but then spent plenty of days not floating and just hiking and hunting. So he mentioned Alaska in general, and then also packrafting in particular, which is important to consider. You know, the more we've hunted Alaska, we just realized Alaska isn't Alaska. The seasons change, the locations change, the context changes, and that maybe changes your approach. So I would just say you can't generalize everything to Alaska. You really do have to look at where and when and how you were hunting in Alaska. So I'll keep my answers mostly to this context of it was early September, interior Alaska, and then also that packrafting element. He asked about first down or synthetic. And on my first read, I thought, well, I don't know if he's talking about clothing or sleep systems. So I'll touch on both. I think he's getting at clothing though, because he also mentioned merino or synthetic, which I'm assuming, you know, is for base layers. So sleep system, just to get that one out of the way, I took my Katabetic Alsec quilt, which is a down quilt. And I mean, if you guys have been listening to the podcast, I've been using this thing for years. Absolutely love it. It's pretty much what I always take on everything. It's just a do it all sleep system for me. So I did take that. And again, it's down. I've taken it to Alaska in many different places and times. And it's been fine. So I know a lot of guys have apprehension about taking down to Alaska. And if that's true for you, I'm not saying you should ignore your apprehension. But I would push back on the idea that synthetic is the only option for a sleep system in Alaska. I actually just recorded a podcast with a guy who hunts in Alaska, yes, but also does insane, insane winter adventures in Alaska. And he uses a down sleep system. And we talk a bit more about that in his context as well. So different episode to come. But I've used down sleep systems in Alaska quite a bit. As always, it's just understanding how do you take care of a down sleep system. In Alaska, yes, but also just in general. So how are you storing it? Do you have a dry bag to protect it if needed, etc. So, yep, that's the sleep system. Katabatic Alsec Down Quilt. In terms of clothes, I took a synthetic puffy and a down puffy on this trip, which is really unique. I generally don't take two of anything from a clothing perspective, except for socks and occasionally boxers. But the idea of taking two puffies is incredibly rare for me. But with it being a pack rafting trip, we did have the ability to have a bit of gear that we weren't always carrying on our back. We're pack rafting. And then also having this idea of, hey, we know we're going to leave the rafts and some extra gear at the river, even if we go backpack for a few days. And so it just created an opportunity to carry a bit more, much like you would if you were hunting with pack animals, for example, on a horseback hunt. So that is a unique aspect of just having the pack raft, as we did take a few extras with us. The synthetic piece that I took for the puffy was a Sitka Kelvin light. I brought that for a few reasons. One is just the ability of if you're on the water and it's cold, which even if the air temp's not cold, like you just get kind of chilled on the water in general, pack rafting, between the wind and a little bit of moisture. And I specifically brought the Kelvin light because it isn't an overly heavy, overly warm, overly insulated piece. But it's, as I mentioned, synthetic. So in my head, it was like, man, this would be a great layer for pack rafting when it's cold. And then also to take on the mountain as well. I'm just looking at the specs. It has 60 grams of insulation in the body, 40 grams of insulation in the sleeves, in the hood. And then basically the side panels and underneath your arms, isn't an insulated synthetic insulation. It's more of like a fleece with a different face that creates more breathability. To be honest with you guys, I love this piece. It was perfect for this trip. And I used it for pack rafting a little bit, but I just used it for the entire trip. Everything from the chilly nights at camp to carrying it on the mountain with me. I had also brought, as I mentioned, that down puffy, which is the Outdoor Vitals Vantage Alpine Down. Been using that for years. And it's a great down puffy jacket. I never touched it. That Sitka Kelvin light, it was truly just the perfect piece for this trip from temperature and moisture and everything else. It was nice to be able to have a puffy that wasn't necessarily as warm and also had some breathability with those side panels and underarms of not quickly overheating if you're moving. I talk about this a lot of not moving in a heavily insulated piece. But there's times where maybe we had it on, our puffy's on and was glassing. And we're just going to make a small move to a different glassing point or to close the distance or something. And just being able to hike a little bit in that Kelvin light was really nice. And then also with it being synthetic, as well as having a pretty good shell and DWR on it, it really did kind of beat and shed water well. And because it was synthetic, I was a little bit less concerned with it immediately, you know, wetting out and losing loft. So there was numerous examples, really. But one in particular, when I killed my bear, it was raining just a little bit when I shot. By the time I got up to the bear, started breaking down the bear. It had started raining a bit more. And it was chilly. I mean, we were high on the mountain in the wind and rain. And I think I eventually put on a rain jacket, but just that ability to have some insulation on and be in a wet environment with some moisture coming down on you and not immediately being concerned of loss of insulation and then trying to dry out wet insulation in the hours or days to come. It was nice. So it's pretty, pretty rare that I'm packing synthetic insulation either to sleep in or to wear. But this trip in particular, man, it was just the perfect choice to have that Kelvin light. Camden also mentioned merino versus synthetic. Again, I'm just assuming he's talking about base layers in general. I would, I mean, you could talk about base layer tops. You could talk about underwear or boxers being synthetic merino. You could talk about base layer bottoms or long johns, et cetera. I'll just stick to base layer tops. I used to use merino a lot more. And in the last, five years, I've used synthetic more than merino. And then I recently got a merino piece from Brad over at Argali. And I just went to look it up and it must not be out yet. I thought it was, but it's not on his clothing page currently. So it must be coming. But I wore an Argali merino hoodie for the duration of the trip. And it was, it was really good. Um, it did not lose shape, which is a problem I've had with a bunch of merino pieces. It was not fragile. It didn't get any holes. It didn't get any pilling. Um, I actually really, really liked it from a fit and durability perspective. You know, the big trade-offs is most of you know with merino versus synthetic is a merino is, uh, known to have less scent, which, you know, on a 10-day hunt is great. and then it's known to not be cold when wet. So in those situations where you do have moisture in the system or you just flat out get wet, merino is not going to have that cold, clammy feeling that synthetic does. On the flip side, it's not going to dry as quickly. So it'll hold moisture longer. Um, and you know, those trade-offs are just true in general. Um, obviously merino, anytime you're talking about merino, you do have to get a little bit nitty-gritty and look at most merino these days are going to be a blend, especially those marketed, targeted towards hunters. They're going to be a merino blend. So there's still some sort of synthetic fiber in there. The question becomes how much? And then obviously merino itself, they're not all the same types and they're not all the same qualities. But, um, yeah, so warmer merino for a base top on this trip and it did stay, um, scent free, essentially. I mean, it didn't get funky or nasty. It was warm in the times that I got a bit wet. There was days that we were hiking and I kind of purposely was hiking without rain gear just because we were exerting and climbing and I would have been wet from the inside out if I was wearing rain gear and I purposely just stayed in that base layer top knowing I was going to get a little bit wet. Um, I did find that going back to having that synthetic puffy, if my base layer got damp, not maybe completely soaking wet but damp, my strategy was always when I stop hiking or moving, especially if we're fully stopping for a bit to glass, even if I'm not cold, I'm going to put that synthetic puffy on and it's just going to allow my body to trap some of the heat that I've generated from just recently hiking and essentially cook myself or warm myself a bit with that merino in part to dry it out. Again, I wasn't putting this puffy on because I was cold yet. I probably would have been cold if I sat there in the wind while I was wet but I'm putting the puffy on proactively to trap and generate warmth to also then help me dry that base layer. I take that same approach with a synthetic base layer. It just happens quicker. So with merino I'm a little more conscious to do it just to try and accelerate that drying out of a merino base layer but hopefully that helps. Camden also mentioned just other tips on staying warm and dry. I guess I just mentioned some. One is don't hike. with too much clothes on in general and then also potentially don't hike with rain gear just knowing hey if it's raining a bit I may get wet obviously without rain gear but even with rain gear I'm probably getting wet depending again on temperatures and conditions and exertion level and so it can be better just to let yourself especially on like that the upper end of your body get a little bit wet and then either as I said stop and dry yourself out when you do stop or have a dry layer such as like a mid layer fleece in your pack that you can just completely change into when you do stop. It's pretty common. I mean the number of times I've been I would say in Alaska yes but this applies to other contexts but just kind of hiking in the rain or not even the rain but like hiking through really wet brush where I will be hiking and climbing with rain pants on over my bottom layer and having those heavily vented to try and let heat escape but to prevent that direct moisture contact from heavy brush and vegetation so I do have some more protection down low with rain pants on but then I'm in my base layer up top and essentially anytime I stop I'm trying to insulate to dry or just change out of that base layer and into a dry mid layer that's such a common strategy so sometimes it's not about hey staying warm and dry it's about getting warm and dry and what's the best way to do that so sometimes again I'm purposely letting myself get a little bit wet knowing that I have a strategy to get warm and dry that is better in the long run so dry layer cooking yourself dry etc the other one is sleeping yourself dry so if it's the end of the day climbing into that sleep system with you know some damp layers it doesn't feel great but it is effective to dry yourself out overnight and yes you can do that with down I'm not saying get swimming in a river soaking wet and do that but yeah it does work especially with just kind of damp moisture overall there were a couple nights as well where it was just crazy crazy wet and we were back by the river at camp and we were able to make a fire which is obviously a fantastic way to get warm and dry we rarely have fires because we're mostly backpack hunting but in the situations where you do get a fire at night it's pretty epic especially after you've been truly wet and cold and tired and hungry and all the things which happened on this trip the other thing when I think of like staying warm and dry or getting warm and dry just to focus on hands and feet and taking care of your hands and feet when I think of the ways I get cold it's at the extremities right so managing moisture and temperature at the hands and feet is important so basic stuff like changing out socks into dry socks when you can usually once a day on a wet hunt not necessarily as a dry hunt for me but on a wet hunt I'm trying to change out at least once a day and then again going back to this being a packraft hunt we packed some extras we did have some of those little boot dryers the fans I have some from Graxaw as well as Mountain Hunter Outdoors that just plug directly into a power bank with a USB-C cable that's integrated into the boot dryers and they work well I mean they're not going to take a soaking wet boot and make it completely bone dry in a short period of time but there's no doubt that they help evacuate moisture out of the boot system and then as you do that just take extra precautions like sometimes at random at night I would pull the insoles out of my boots I'd put the insoles in the bottom of my sleeping bag again to have body warmth help dry those out and then run the fans a bit through the boot without the insoles yeah it definitely makes a difference we talked about didn't do it on this trip but doing more again it's rare we pack these boot dryers but on a hunt like this they're great and then next time we would also bring some hot hands packets and then try to accelerate okay now we can move some warm air through the boots with a hot hands packet and that would be even more effective so that's feet you know boot moisture and socks and I'll talk more about boots in a second because we had a question on it and then on hands obviously just thinking of gloves you know gloves are tricky with what type of glove what's the context do you need something with durability for thorns and brush not uncommon in Alaska do you need something with dexterity maybe maybe not depending on what you're doing can you go maximum warmth with something like a down glove because you're sitting in glassing but it doesn't have dexterity or durability so there's lots of you know use cases and contexts for gloves but one I just wanted to highlight as well because this believe it or not does help with warmth but definitely helps with moisture it's just wearing nitrile gloves at times you know some guys are packing these to break down an animal I kind of go 50-50 on if I wear them to pack out an animal but there's some specific ones I pack that I think I got this from my buddy Tyler Boshma who was also on this hunt but got it years ago some orange nitrile gloves that have these really good gripper dots they are great for breaking down an animal but more and more on wet hunts there's times where I'm just putting those nitrile gloves on if I'm hiking or packing out just to help keep those hands a bit warm and dry and even though they're you know thin and they're not an insulation layer if you will they are helpful with warmth and then I will also put them under other gloves at times almost like a vapor barrier or layer and I did that while pack rafting as well so yeah those nitrile gloves can be actually quite helpful and then other tips on staying warm and dry again just I could we could talk forever and do an in-depth podcast but the other one I would say is just taking care of your gear making sure that things stay dry not just when they're on your body but at camp or in your pack or in this case when we were pack rafting you know having dry bags and just making sure you're paying attention and on wet trips everything is moisture management and it's really a holistic approach it's like while you're hiking while you're hunting while you're in camp while you're in our case pack rafting while you're doing anything are you keeping things dry when you can are you drying things out that got wet when you can it's just kind of you're paying constant attention to everything and that is gonna pay off really all right next question from Dylan he said Mark I know you love the Hanwag Albertstones but they appear to be discontinued or something do you happen to know if a new version is coming is there something else you have been using instead so yeah the Hanwag Albertstones I've been using for many years again if you listen to the podcast you probably know that they have been a phenomenal phenomenal boot for me from a comfort fit performance perspective they just work for my feet they work in all conditions I never tape I never blister I never do anything they're awesome I have had some issues with durability on them and then mediocre to mixed customer service from Hanwag on an issue with durability it really goes back to 2023 I had a new pair of Oliver stones that I purposely had done minimal minimal use with because I don't find that for me these boots need much break-in and I took them on a sheep hunt backpack sheep hunt and they failed like pretty dang quickly and then yeah it was a kind of a headache to get them replaced anyway I still have that failed pair which I fixed I still have a brand new replacement pair which I got from Hanwag and I still have an older previous pair of the boots that were the boots I was using before that kind of sheep hunt so I have three pairs of them from quite used to not too used but had some issues to brand new and so I still do use them but because I know that they did go away I've been looking for an alternative I to be honest with you I have zero affiliation with Hanwag but I would I had such good results with these boots except for that durability issue which hopefully was a one-off but they just work so well for me that I just wouldn't consider anything else if they still made them but because they did stop making them I have been looking at other things and doing testing and I've tested quite a few alternatives I haven't talked much on the podcast about it because I hadn't really found something that I loved until actually pretty recently the Mindel what they call their air revolution alpine light hiker it's a mouthful but if you look it's a Mindel alpine light hiker essentially has been a really good boot that I've used I used on this recent Alaska trip I used last spring in a bear hunt and then going back to last fall I used it some as well so about a year now have I been using that boot and it's been really really good it's kind of a what they have in their flex line of an a b so a has the least flex so it's like a b flex it's a it's not a uber flexible boot but it's not an uber stiff boot it's kind of that good in between pretty similar to an Oliverstone it's built well you know it's been great from a durability perspective for me the outsole's pretty good with grip the leasing systems really nice on it yeah it's been waterproof I mentioned earlier getting boots wet you know there's times where the boot itself was dry from an external boot perspective but because of going back to like my strategy I talked about hiking in rain gear there's times I didn't have it and then got wet pants and then the wet pants seep down and eventually work some way into having moisture in the boot system it just happens but the boots themselves have from a boot exterior keeping water out have been waterproof yeah so they've been great they've been really good check them out you know if you're kind of interested in that kind of like they call a light hiker an early season boot but again my preference and it's to you similar even more so of just what most people call an early season boot we would call an all season boot and what most people would call a light hiker we would just call boot like that's the type of boot we tend to prefer so yeah it's been good I will say a note on sizing is exact opposite of the alver stones where I used to size up I'm mostly an 11 and a half and on the alver stones I went up to a 12 and actually a 12 wide whereas with the mindles I go down to an 11 they do talk on their site about most people needing to size down half size which was the case for me and then they also have a really cool app basically like if you just go to their website and do their sizing thing it asks you to use your phone and like scan your foot and take pictures and do this thing and actually found that it works really well before about the boots and it again it the their app recommended an 11 after me doing the process and scanning my foot and I was hesitant I can't remember the last time I wore a size 11 in a boot but it was the right call for these so that's a good one then while I'm on the subject of boots this is personal and probably like somewhat unique but I have to throw this out there I have been dealing with an issue where my left toe essentially my ring finger but my toe my left kind of toe next to my pinky would occasionally cramp slash have a terrible pain slash feel a tingly nummy burny type sensation and I've dealt with it for a few years and it's gotten worse mostly when I'm hiking and hunting mostly when I'm in boots thankfully I don't get it day to day some people do I essentially narrowed it down talked to some other people actually Justin who works for XO Josh Kuntz who's been on the podcast they actually both deal with it as well Morton's neuroma essentially is what the issue is in my case and it can have like some different symptoms but me Josh and Justin I'll deal with it a little bit and different again symptoms aren't always the same but it has been incredibly annoying to me and the ways that it would come on and how honestly how debilitating it was because there was times where it was such an acute nerve pain it completely changed my gait and made it hard for me to walk normally hike normally etc and man it has been such a pain in the butt I would try different insoles different boots different things I just dealt with it until once I figured out one it was Morton's neuroma and then two looked at some treatments for that their insoles with what most people call like a met pad and essentially it is a hump in the insole that comes up and puts pressure on the foot between the arch and your toes not quite the ball of the foot but in that area and it basically is forcing your toes to be able to like arc a little bit and then also splay out a bit so I've been using those just this year and I just did this 10 day hunt in Alaska and had I mean if yeah I just had such little issue and symptom and just like the the slightest slightest touch was almost non-existent of dealing with this problem because those insoles so I'll leave a link to the ones I use there's several out there I actually would say these insoles are not nearly as comfortable as a lot of standard insoles I've used like we've talked a lot about the hike cushion insoles from super feet they're not that comfortable so my feet do get more tired with these metatarsal insoles but it solves that Morton's neuroma issue for me so it's been worth the trade-off so if someone out there by chance is dealing with something like this definitely try insoles with a metatarsal pad in there all right next question is from Nick he said I've seen your sharpening video and the diamond sharper that you use and recommend but I was wondering if you had any suggestions for a sharpener with an angle guide all right so I will leave a link in the show description to the sharpening video and the sharpener I use in the video in case people are hearing this I'm wondering what the heck Nick is talking about but the sharpener I use does not have an angle guide which is why he's asking so the key to sharpening you know freehand on a flat stone or surface is consistency with angle like that is the key you have to keep an angle and you have to keep it consistently and that angle should then therefore match your knife as well so if you can do that keep a consistent angle with your knife that makes sharpening easy but it can be a struggle especially if you're newer to sharpening so the idea of an angle guide is it puts your knife in the correct position before you start to you know sharpen it and move it along the surface it's just putting you in that proper start position and then you just maintain that position I will say anytime you're using something with an angle guide it's only going to be helpful if that angle matches the angle of your knife and how the edge on your knife was not designed per se but the pre existing angle of your blade basically so not all blades have the same blade angle and you don't have to be perfect like in theory if you had a 20 degree angle guide which I believe the sharpener I'm about to mention has and your specific blade was 19 or 18 like you're gonna be fine but obviously if you start to look at knives in general you can get quite a bit of variance in angles the good news though is a lot of hunting knives organ they're going to tend to be around that 20 degree angle it's different when you get into tools heavy use knives or cutlery you know cutlery kitchen knives that type of thing the angles can vary but thankfully most hunting knives are going to be similar ish but I just did want to throw that out there if you try an angle guide and it doesn't work well perhaps it just doesn't match your blade you can re profile a blade so you can change the angle of a blade but it takes a bit more work and usually some different grits and tools and whole different discussion but to get back to the next question after getting derailed what sharpener could I recommend with an angle guide and I would say in particular making this recommendation for something you can take on your hunt there's plenty of sharpening systems that work great at home that you can have an angle guide or even set the angle a lot of adjustable angle sharpeners that are great at home but in terms of taking something into the field and then also keeping it compact and relatively lightweight so you could take it on a backpack hunt or backcountry hunt work sharp has what they call their folding field sharpener they have a few different field sharpeners some are a bit bigger and have more features and edges and options the folding field sharpener is the one that I can recommend from a con a compact lightweight perspective it has both a diamond grit you know diamond surface and then also a ceramic surface so you essentially have like two options here it's going to work really well and this is kind of true of all sharpening just keep in mind the purpose especially in the field of sharpening a knife shouldn't be to take an incredibly dull crappy edge on your knife and make it sharp it should be to sharpen an edge that is beginning to lose its edge think of sharpening in the field as maintenance not like rehab right it's I'm gonna take care of my knife as I'm working on an animal not completely beat the crap out of my knife and then try and you know work a miracle in the field so if you do that and actually did use this sharpener on this recent Alaska trip for example and I killed one bear but you know we filled four tags and we all pitch in and break down animals so essentially it was part of breaking down four animals the sharpener is great it was really good and again it has that angle guide so that's my long answer to next question what angle guide sharpener should you take on a hunt check out the workshop folding field sharpener I think it's 30 bucks maybe side note as well while we're talking knives I tried a new knife on this trip which you know try a lot and we just get access to a lot of knives or people like hey try this check this out from Alaska blade works just kind of met those guys their customers of XO and you know mentioned they had the knife company and all that stuff so I used it on this hunt again part of breaking down for animals the model I had was their wrangle great knife really good knife there's a lot of good knives out there if you're shopping check this one out too it was genuinely really really good so check that out if you'd like for all this stuff I know I mentioned like the sharpening video I was leaving links to but pretty much all this gear I've mentioned for the most part everything from let's see the boots to the nitrile gloves to the boot dryers to the jackets whatever I'll leave links to all that stuff so check out the links in the description and as always nothing sponsored or affiliate or kickbacks or whatever just our genuine takes all right let's do one more Kevin Kevin says perhaps this is an odd question but what phone case do you guys use I'm looking for something rugged enough for drops and to carry in the mountains but I don't want it to be too bulky and ideally something that would also work well with a digiscoping setup yeah Kevin I don't it's probably not super odd but it is I don't think we've ever talked about phone cases before the one I'm currently using and really like is from rock form are okay rock rock form and it is I think it would fit well for Kevin he mentioned something that's you know rugged but not too bulky it's definitely the case it's on the more rugged end of cases has good edges and like bumpers and button protection and raised edges around the camera so it's rugged from that perspective it's not crazy bulky and then one thing for me that's like a pet peeve with phone cases is the grip so I want something grippy and not super slick but I also don't like cases that are sticky so I don't like when the material is like too rubbery grippy but then it is a pain in the butt getting it in and out of your pocket because it's gripping when you don't want it to but then I also hate when I have my phone in my hand and the phone is slick or too thin or doesn't have any like texture or edge to it and then it just slips out of my hand more so the rock form is I would say is a good balance of that it's a harder material so not uber sticky rubbery but it has grip and texture and ridges and things like that that make it to me something with yeah good grip to it what I really like about the rock form which I didn't realize I was gonna love so much is that it does it has the mag safe ring which you know I think has been standard on my iPhones for quite a long time it has that but it has another magnet below it that's really strong and this sounds really dumb but I use it all the time to just be able to put my phone on things the mag safe I've found you can't just like throw your phone on a piece of metal and expect the phone to hold but with this extra magnet my phone does so it I mean yeah all the time sort of like in the garage I just put my phone on the storage shelf and it sticks up there in the gym all the time I was just at the gym this morning when I'm at the weight rack or doing squats or something it's just set my phone up on the weight rack and it's just holding so it's one of those things I didn't expect to use or be a benefit but now I think I would really struggle and constantly be putting my phone on things and having it fall off if it didn't have that extra magnet and strength and then he mentioned ideally something with a digiscope setup there's obviously so many digiscoping setups these days some you have to use a specific phone case some of them you have to add something to your phone case there's lots of different approaches the epic mountain mag system uses the mag safe ring that's part of an iphone in most iphone cases and does work well with this rock form case again there's not it's not a unique integration between rock form and the mountain mag it's just using that pretty common mag safe ring and works well so that's what I've been doing you know in years past I have used all in and blanking on the other super original kind of giant digiscoping adapter phone scope sorry where you had to use different cases for your phone and I ideally just don't want to have to mess with that anymore just have one case and it works for digiscoping and so I'm using again this rock form case with the mountain mag system well that's a wrap it there guys we will be back to answer more questions we'll get Steve back to help us answer more questions so let us know what you want to hear send an email to podcast at xomontagear.com for that for the things I mentioned in today's episode I'll leave links in the show description and then just be sure to also hit subscribe or follow in your podcast app so that you get future episodes we'll have a full length episode coming on Wednesday we've got some great stuff coming in the lineup some interviews that I've really enjoyed and I'm excited to share with you so yeah thanks as always to you guys if you have any questions comments feedback in addition to a Monday minute question if you have a guest suggestion something we should do a full length episode on someone we should have on the show reach out let us know it'd be great to hear from you and we'll talk to you soon
